  {
    "text": "Yes. Paroxetine is a strong inhibitor of CYP2D6, and metoprolol is metabolised principally by CYP2D6, with extensive first-pass metabolism dependent on this enzyme. Co-administration means paroxetine will inhibit metoprolol's CYP2D6-mediated metabolism, reducing both first-pass clearance and systemic clearance. This increases metoprolol plasma concentrations, which can produce exaggerated beta-blockade — bradycardia, hypotension, dizziness — and this interaction is clinically recognised as significant. Dose reduction of metoprolol or switching to a non-CYP2D6-dependent beta-blocker is typically advised.\n\nMECHANISM: Paroxetine strongly inhibits CYP2D6, decreasing metabolism of metoprolol (a CYP2D6 substrate) and raising its exposure\nFINAL ANSWER: YES",
